  "rationale": "The late-career masterpiece of an architect of the music: an octet of four saxophones (Carter and Phil Woods on alto, Coleman Hawkins and Charlie Rouse on tenor) with piano, guitar, bass and drums, deliberately re-creating the instrumentation of Carter's 1937 Paris session with Hawkins and Django Reinhardt and then surpassing it (S1, S2). Penguin Guide selects it for its Core Collection; the Rolling Stone Jazz Record Guide gives it five stars; DownBeat gave the original LP four and a half (S1). AllMusic calls it a classic and 'very highly recommended' and notes it was Carter's only small-group recording of the era (S2). Lineage is the argument: Hawkins and Carter from the swing era, Rouse and Woods at the centre of the 1960s music, Garrison about to join Coltrane, Jo Jones the pulse of the whole tradition — a record that explains how the music connects (S1, S3).",
