    "tier": "consensus_core",
    "priority": "strong",
    "case_for": "Penguin Guide Core Collection selection, five stars from the Rolling Stone Jazz Record Guide, and four-and-a-half from DownBeat establish substantial critical consensus, although these judgments are reported through S1; AllMusic independently calls it a classic and highly recommends it (S2). Carter's four-saxophone arrangements make the intergenerational personnel more than an all-star attraction: Carter and Coleman Hawkins meet Phil Woods and Charlie Rouse, supported by a rhythm section including Jimmy Garrison and Jo Jones (S3). Revisiting the instrumentation of Carter's 1937 Paris session, the album makes the continuity between swing and modern jazz audible through concise, immediately approachable performances (S1, S2).",
    "case_against": "The strongest objection is that this is principally a triumph of mainstream swing arranging rather than a defining advance in post-bebop: its standards-heavy repertoire and compact, polished charts consolidate an inherited language more than establish a new one. The celebrated personnel demonstrate historical connection, but their presence alone does not establish that this particular album materially shaped subsequent post-bop development. The consensus case also needs a provenance qualification: three of its strongest critical endorsements are mediated by Wikipedia rather than directly documented in the dossier.",
    "disagreement": null,
    "recommendation": "The council proposes that John give Further Definitions strong priority as a consensus-backed demonstration of the living connection between swing and modern jazz.",
